{
  "summary": "The previous critiques are strong but overcomplicate the solution. Optimistic UI and server-side waiting both add significant complexity for marginal gain. The pragmatic fix is two changes: (1) For slow initial load, call smartPollFeatures() immediately on DOMContentLoaded AND reduce the initial poll interval to 1s for the first 10 seconds (then revert to 5s). This handles both the 'pipeline already connected but no state_update yet' case and the 'pipeline not connected yet' case without any server changes. A CSS loading skeleton is unnecessary — the page already renders empty stage sections from the Go template; the real problem is just the 5-second gap before the first poll fires. (2) For the post-action race condition affecting ALL mutations (create_idea, move, edit-title, edit-type, edit-description, edit-done-script, edit-depends-on), replace the scattered setTimeout(smartPollFeatures, 500) pattern with a single helper like pollUntilChanged(maxRetries=4, intervalMs=750) that calls smartPollFeatures repeatedly until _lastFeaturesHTML actually changes or retries are exhausted. This is deterministic enough — if the HTML hasn't changed after 3 seconds, the pipeline genuinely hasn't processed the action yet, and the next regular 5s poll will catch it. This avoids the complexity of optimistic UI (which requires client-side state management, pending indicators, and reconciliation logic for every action type), avoids coupling HTTP responses to WebSocket round-trips on the server, and requires zero Go server changes — it's purely client-side JavaScript."
